--- GATE ANALYSIS: Why positive controls may not fire ---
[21:47:24]   The Eisenbud-Schreyer theorem guarantees that REGULAR sequences have
[21:47:24]   HF matching the semiregular prediction EXACTLY (no strict early fall).
[21:47:24]   Non-regular systems can have d_ff < D_reg_pred ONLY if the actual HF
[21:47:24]   drops below the positive-truncated semiregular series.
[21:47:24]   Key cases where a non-regular system may STILL match the semiregular HF:
[21:47:24]   (a) The system is zero-dimensional AND happens to be a CI (regular). 
[21:47:24]   (b) The extra syzygies cancel in a way that preserves the HF profile.
[21:47:24]   (c) The system is positive-dimensional: HF at D_reg_pred > 0 (late fall).
[21:47:24]   For P-syz ({xy-1,xz-1,yz-1}):
[21:47:24]     ideal dimension = 0
[21:47:24]     gb degs = [1, 1, 2]
[21:47:24]   If dim=0 and GB degs match the semiregular profile, the system IS effectively
[21:47:24]   regular even though it has syzygies (they cancel in the Hilbert series).
[21:47:24]   CONCLUSION: The early-fall criterion (d_ff < D_reg_pred) is strictly HARDER
[21:47:24]   to satisfy than 'has syzygies'. What matters is whether the Hilbert function
[21:47:24]   deviates from the semiregular series -- which requires SIGNIFICANT syzygies.
[21:47:24] 
